---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Best Walking Machines for Seniors (2026): 6 Picks by Balance";
const description =
  "The Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill ($339 on Amazon, 28 Nov; 0.3 mph start, full-length rails) suits most seniors; a seated stepper suits poor balance.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "exerpeutic-senior-fitness-treadmill",
    name: "Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill",
    brand: "Exerpeutic",
    category: "Treadmill with full-length side rails",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0DJB6N28H?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41s4hBtFBAL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $399 (Amazon, 6 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":399,"high":399},
    specs: "Full-length padded side rails plus front rail · 0.3–4 mph in 0.1 mph steps · 43.5 x 16 in. belt · 400 lb capacity · manual incline",
    pros: [
      "Padded rails run the length of the belt on both sides, with a second bar at the front",
      "A 0.3 mph start, the slowest on this page, rising 0.1 mph at a time",
      "Four main controls — start, stop, faster, slower — and a backlit display",
      "A 400 lb rating gives a wide margin for most users",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 4 mph ceiling: walking only, by design",
      "The 16-inch belt is narrower than a full-size treadmill’s",
      "The listing says the step-up is low but gives no figure, and the manual incline has no stated range",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The walking machine most older walkers should start with: rails within reach for the whole walk, a belt that barely creeps at first, and nothing to learn.",
    description: "This is a treadmill designed for the reader of this page rather than adapted for them. By its Amazon listing, padded side rails run the full length of the belt on both sides, and a further rail crosses the front, so you can walk in the middle of the deck with a hand down at your side instead of reaching forward to a console. The belt starts at 0.3 mph and moves up in 0.1 mph steps to a 4 mph maximum, which is about as slow a start as any home treadmill offers. The console is reduced to start, stop, faster and slower, with a backlit display of time, distance, calories, speed and pulse. The listing gives a 43.5 by 16-inch belt, six deck cushions, a manual incline and a 400 lb maximum user weight. The limits follow from the design: it is for walking only, the belt is narrower than a full-size treadmill's, and the incline is set by hand with no range given. The listing describes a low step-up without a number, so measure or ask if stepping up is your concern. Amazon listed it at $399 on 6 November 2026, when our handrail guide checked it.",
  },
  {
    id: "teeter-freestep-lt3",
    name: "Teeter FreeStep LT3 Recumbent Cross Trainer",
    brand: "Teeter",
    category: "Seated cross trainer (recumbent stepper)",
    badge: "Best Seated",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0C7F7JHS3?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/410DvO0SZCL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$859–$1,030 (with mat on Amazon)",
    priceBounds: {"low":859,"high":1030},
    specs: "13 magnetic resistance levels · 300 lb max user · 110 lb unit weight · seat adjusts for depth and height, 3-position recline · 4-position handles",
    pros: [
      "No belt and no step up: you sit down and push the pedals in a stepping motion",
      "Arms and legs work together through linked handles",
      "The seat moves for depth and height and reclines, so most people can find a comfortable position",
      "The console runs on batteries, so it can go anywhere in the room",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Not weight-bearing, so it does less for bones and walking balance than standing",
      "The seat does not swivel; you step in beside it, which some users find awkward",
      "Teeter and a dealer disagree on its footprint and warranty, and hands-on testing is thin",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The machine to choose when a moving belt is a risk: the stepping action of walking, done sitting down, at a home price.",
    description: "The FreeStep is the home version of the seated steppers used in physiotherapy clinics, and the LT3 is the middle of Teeter's three models. You sit in a reclining seat and push the pedals forward one after the other in a straight-line stepping motion, while handles linked to the pedals move your arms; Teeter says the motion is licensed from commercial physiotherapy steppers. For an older person who should not be on a treadmill, it keeps the alternating leg action of walking without any of the risk of a moving floor. Teeter and Northern Fitness, a dealer, agree on 13 levels of magnetic resistance, a 300 lb user capacity and a weight of about 110 lb. The seat adjusts for depth and height and reclines to three positions, the handles have four positions, and the console, which shows time, distance, speed and calories, runs on two AA batteries. Teeter and the dealer give different footprints and warranty terms, so check both on the listing you buy from. Teeter had it from $899 in October and an Amazon snapshot from about $859; on 9 November 2026 the Amazon listing we link sold it with a floor mat at $1,029.99. If getting into the seat is the difficulty, the dearer LT7 adds a Step Lock that holds the pedals and handles still.",
  },
  {
    id: "sunny-treadpad-7-auto-incline",
    name: "Sunny Health & Fitness Treadpad Treadmill 7% Auto Incline",
    brand: "Sunny Health & Fitness",
    category: "Walking pad with a front handlebar",
    badge: "Best Walking Pad",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0BM2SRJ9D?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/417uQ6wp8XL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $487 (Amazon, 6 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":487,"high":487},
    specs: "45.7 x 16 in. running surface · 0.5–3.7 mph handlebar down, to 6 mph handlebar up · 7% auto incline · 265 lb capacity (Sunny)",
    pros: [
      "A deck 4.1 inches off the floor by Sunny’s figure, about half the step of a full-size treadmill",
      "A front handlebar that folds up, and a 0.5 mph starting speed",
      "A 45.7-inch walking surface, long for a pad",
      "Sunny publishes its weight limit, unit weight and deck height on its own store",
    ],
    cons: [
      "One bar at the front, not rails along the sides",
      "At 79.4 lb, heavy to move for a pad",
      "The incline raises the deck to 5.7 inches; leave it flat for getting on and off",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The compact choice for a steady walker short of space: a low deck, a bar to hold and a slow start, in a machine that folds away.",
    description: "Most walking pads are a bad idea for an older walker because there is nothing to hold. Sunny's Treadpad is one of the exceptions: a low pad with a handlebar that folds up at the front. Sunny's own store gives a deck height of 4.1 inches with the pad flat and 5.7 inches at full incline, a 45.7 by 16-inch running surface, a 265 lb maximum user weight and a 79.4 lb product weight, and the Amazon listing agrees on the surface and speeds. With the bar folded down it walks at 0.5 to 3.7 mph; with the bar up it can reach 6 mph, though an older walker rarely needs that. A powered incline to 7% in six steps adds effort without adding speed, and there is a remote and the SunnyFit app. The honest limit is the bar itself: it is one handle in front of you, which steadies you when you start and stop but does not give you something to hold at your side for the whole walk the way the Exerpeutic's rails do. Amazon showed it at $487.26 on 6 November 2026. Sunny's store gives three years on the frame and 180 days on other parts.",
  },
  {
    id: "sunny-low-deck-treadmill-extended-handrails",
    name: "Sunny Health & Fitness Low Deck Treadmill with Extended Safety Handrails",
    brand: "Sunny Health & Fitness",
    category: "Low-deck treadmill with extended rails",
    badge: "Lowest Step-Up",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0BV5FMX74?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/414YnsLq55L._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $680 (Amazon, 6 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":680,"high":680},
    specs: "4 in. floor-to-deck height (listing and Sunny) · 47.2 x 18.9 in. running surface (Sunny) · up to 8.5 mph · 245 lb capacity (Sunny)",
    pros: [
      "A 4-inch step onto the deck, stated by both the listing and Sunny",
      "Rails extend along the deck as well as across the front",
      "The widest belt of the treadmills here, at 18.9 inches",
      "Pulse sensors and large console buttons",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 245 lb weight limit, the lowest on this page",
      "Dearer than the Exerpeutic, at about $680",
      "Not a clinical machine, and the listing does not say how it folds",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a walker whose real difficulty is the step up: a kerb-height deck with rails to hold along it, and a wider belt than the budget rail treadmills.",
    description: "Where the Exerpeutic solves the problem of holding on, this Sunny solves the problem of getting on. Its Amazon listing and Sunny's store both give a floor-to-deck height of 4 inches, which is a kerb rather than a stair, and rails extend along the sides of the deck as well as across the front. Sunny's matching model on its own store (SF-T722062) lists a 47.2 by 18.9-inch running surface — wider than any other treadmill here — a 2.5 HP peak motor, a top speed of 8.5 mph, a 245 lb maximum user weight and a 115.5 lb product weight. The listing adds a safety clip, a non-slip deck, shock absorption, pulse sensors, large console buttons and Bluetooth to Sunny's app. For someone with a stiff hip, a replaced knee or a short stride, that low step can matter more than anything else on the spec sheet. It is not a rehabilitation treadmill: it has no reverse belt or medical features. Its 245 lb limit is the lowest on this page, so heavier walkers should look elsewhere. Amazon showed it at $679.96 on 6 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "sole-f63",
    name: "Sole F63",
    brand: "Sole",
    category: "Full-size folding treadmill",
    badge: "Best Full-Size",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HFSXYZY4?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31Ka8IzhlWL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":333},
    reviewUrl: "/sole-f63-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "$899-$1,099",
    priceBounds: {"low":899,"high":1099},
    specs: "long side rails · 325 lb capacity · large-digit LED",
    pros: [
      "Side rails that run well down the deck, not just beside the console",
      "Large, high-contrast LED digits that are easy to read mid-walk",
      "Starts without an app, an account or a subscription",
      "A full-length 60-inch deck, per our review, for a natural stride",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Sole does not publish a step-up height, and a full-size deck is higher than a pad’s",
      "Too heavy for one person to move; assembly needs help",
      "Built for a wide range of users, with more speed than a cautious walker needs",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a confident older walker who wants a proper treadmill that will last, with rails to hold and a console they can read.",
    description: "The F63 is here for the walker in the first row of the balance table: steady on their feet, perhaps still walking miles outdoors, and wanting a full treadmill rather than a specialist machine. Its case for an older user is simpler than its case for a runner. The side rails extend well down the deck, so there is something to hold for the whole stride, and the display uses large, high-contrast digits that can be read at a glance. Nothing about starting a walk needs a phone. Our review gives it a full-length 60-inch deck and a 12 mph top speed, far more than a walker needs, and Sole lists a 325 lb capacity. The cautions are the ones that apply to every full-size treadmill: Sole does not publish the step-up height, which will be higher than a walking pad's or the Sunny low-deck's, so check it in a store; the machine is heavy and needs two people to build and move; and it changes speed in bigger steps than the rail treadmills above. Our senior treadmill guide lists it at $899 to $1,099.",
  },
  {
    id: "nustep-t5",
    name: "NuStep T5 Recumbent Cross Trainer",
    brand: "NuStep",
    category: "Clinical seated cross trainer",
    badge: "Best for Limited Mobility",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08NPX5GF1?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41QiBQdcPML._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":346},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "From $7,295",
    priceBounds: {"low":7295,"high":7295},
    specs: "15 resistance levels · 500 lb max user · 360° swivel seat locking every 45° · 18 in. seat travel · fits 4 ft 6 in. to 6 ft 7 in.",
    pros: [
      "The seat swivels a full circle and locks every 45°, so you can sit down sideways and turn in",
      "A 500 lb rating, the highest on this page, agreed by NuStep and a medical dealer",
      "Flip-up arm rests, a grab ring and a low step-through frame",
      "The kind of machine many rehabilitation clinics already use",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Several times the price of every other machine here",
      "Sold direct and through medical dealers; we did not find it new on Amazon",
      "73 inches long and heavy, by NuStep’s figures",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For someone with limited mobility, or continuing rehabilitation at home, where a swivel seat and a clinic-grade frame are worth the price.",
    description: "If the person who will use the machine needs help getting into a chair, or is continuing therapy at home after a stroke, a joint replacement or a long illness, the NuStep T5 is the machine physiotherapists are most likely to know. NuStep introduced its cross trainer in 1995 and designs for clinics and homes alike. NuStep and Tiger Medical, a medical dealer, agree on a 500 lb user capacity, 15 resistance levels, a seat that swivels 360 degrees and locks every 45 degrees, up to 18 inches of seat travel and a user height range of 4 ft 6 in. to 6 ft 7 in. The swivel seat is the point: you sit down facing the side, as you would on a chair, then turn and lock into position. NuStep also lists flip-up arm rests, a grab ring, adjustable arms, contact heart-rate handles and a large color display, with a 10-year frame, three-year parts and one-year labor warranty. It is 73 inches long and weighs 285 lb by NuStep's figure. NuStep listed it from $7,295 plus delivery when our recumbent guide checked it; we did not find it sold new on Amazon, so the button searches for it. Ask a therapist whether a used or ex-clinic unit would suit.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Walking Machines for Seniors", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-walking-machines-for-seniors/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best walking machine for seniors?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most older walkers, a treadmill with full-length side rails and a very slow start, such as the Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill: rails on both sides, a 0.3 mph start and a 400 lb rating for about $399. If balance is poor or you have fallen recently, a seated cross trainer such as the Teeter FreeStep LT3 is the safer choice.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a treadmill or a walking pad better for an elderly person?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A treadmill with full-length rails is better for most elderly walkers, because there is something to hold for the whole walk. A walking pad is lower and smaller, which helps with stiff hips and small rooms, but most have no handrail and short belts. If you choose a pad, choose one with a handrail and a slow start.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a seated walking machine?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually a seated or recumbent cross trainer: you sit in a chair-like seat and push two pedals forward in turn while linked handles move your arms. It copies the alternating leg action of walking without a moving belt or a step up. The Teeter FreeStep and NuStep T5 are the best-known examples.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What walking machine for seniors has the lowest starting speed?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Of the machines on this page, the Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill: it starts at 0.3 mph and rises 0.1 mph at a time to 4 mph, by its Amazon listing. The Sunny Treadpad starts at 0.5 mph. Seated cross trainers such as the Teeter FreeStep have no belt at all, so they move only as fast as you push.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What weight capacity should a treadmill for a senior have?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "At least 50 lb more than the heaviest user. The picks here range from 245 lb on the Sunny low-deck treadmill to 400 lb on the Exerpeutic and 500 lb on the NuStep T5. A higher rating usually means a stiffer frame, which helps a cautious walker feel steady.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill on sale for Black Friday?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It was $339.15 on Amazon on 28 November 2026, about $60 under the $399 it showed on 6 November. Check the brand on the listing before you buy, because its title no longer starts with Exerpeutic and similar senior treadmills from other brands use near-identical titles.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are walking machines safe for people with balance problems?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A moving belt is a fall risk for anyone with poor balance, even with rails and a safety key. If you use a cane or walker, get dizzy on standing or have fallen in the past year, a seated cross trainer is the safer walking machine. Ask a physiotherapist before using any treadmill.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-17",
      dateModified: "2026-11-28",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              ...(product.amazonUrl ? { url: product.amazonUrl } : {}),
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 6,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-walking-machines-for-seniors/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/best-walking-machines-for-seniors/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "best");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const GROUP_PAGES = GROUP?.pages ?? [];
const HERE_AT = GROUP_PAGES.findIndex((p) => p.url === HERE);
// The group's first 2 pages (its leads), then the pages after this one, wrapping round, so every page in a long
// hub group gets sibling links and the leads keep theirs (PLAYBOOK §0b L1).
const MORE = [...new Set([...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, 2), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(HERE_AT + 1), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, Math.max(HERE_AT, 0))])].filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-17",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-28",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best walking machines for seniors",
      "walking machine for elderly",
      "treadmill with handrails for seniors",
      "seated stepper for seniors",
      "walking pad with handrail for seniors",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Best Overall: Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/best/">Best Treadmills</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buying Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Best Walking Machines for Seniors</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        The best walking machine for an older person depends less on the machine than on the walker: how steady they are, how high they can step and whether they need something to hold. Here are six machines across four types, from a full-rail treadmill to a seated stepper, and a simple table for choosing between them.
      </p>
      <p class="ed-answer"><strong>Short answer.</strong> Best Overall: <a href="#exerpeutic-senior-fitness-treadmill">Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill</a>. Best Seated: <a href="#teeter-freestep-lt3">Teeter FreeStep LT3 Recumbent Cross Trainer</a>. Best Walking Pad: <a href="#sunny-treadpad-7-auto-incline">Sunny Health & Fitness Treadpad Treadmill 7% Auto Incline</a>.</p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 28, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">How we chose: makers’ published specifications and manuals, independent reviews and owner reports, cross-checked. Not tested hands-on.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We may earn a commission when you buy through the links below. It costs you nothing and never decides a ranking. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#exerpeutic-senior-fitness-treadmill"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41s4hBtFBAL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#teeter-freestep-lt3"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/410DvO0SZCL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Teeter FreeStep LT3 Recumbent Cross Trainer" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#sunny-treadpad-7-auto-incline"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/417uQ6wp8XL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Sunny Health &amp; Fitness Treadpad Treadmill 7% Auto Incline" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill · Teeter FreeStep LT3 Recumbent Cross Trainer · Sunny Health &amp; Fitness Treadpad Treadmill 7% Auto Incline</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          "Walking machine" covers more than one kind of equipment, and for an older person the
          difference matters more than any feature on a spec sheet. A treadmill with rails along both
          sides lets you walk with a hand on something the whole time. A walking pad sits low to the
          floor and takes up little room, but most have nothing to hold. A seated cross trainer, the kind
          of machine physiotherapy clinics use, moves your legs in a walking-like stepping motion while
          you sit in a chair with a backrest. This guide explains which type suits which walker, then
          names six machines whose figures we could check, from about $400 to over $7,000.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Overall:</span> <a href="#exerpeutic-senior-fitness-treadmill">Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Seated:</span> <a href="#teeter-freestep-lt3">Teeter FreeStep LT3 Recumbent Cross Trainer</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
