        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best walking machine for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most older walkers, a treadmill with full-length side rails and a very slow start, such as the Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill: rails on both sides, a 0.3 mph start and a 400 lb rating for about $399. If balance is poor or you have fallen recently, a seated cross trainer such as the Teeter FreeStep LT3 is the safer choice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill or a walking pad better for an elderly person?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A treadmill with full-length rails is better for most elderly walkers, because there is something to hold for the whole walk. A walking pad is lower and smaller, which helps with stiff hips and small rooms, but most have no handrail and short belts. If you choose a pad, choose one with a handrail and a slow start.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is a seated walking machine?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually a seated or recumbent cross trainer: you sit in a chair-like seat and push two pedals forward in turn while linked handles move your arms. It copies the alternating leg action of walking without a moving belt or a step up. The Teeter FreeStep and NuStep T5 are the best-known examples.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What walking machine for seniors has the lowest starting speed?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Of the machines on this page, the Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill: it starts at 0.3 mph and rises 0.1 mph at a time to 4 mph, by its Amazon listing. The Sunny Treadpad starts at 0.5 mph. Seated cross trainers such as the Teeter FreeStep have no belt at all, so they move only as fast as you push.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What weight capacity should a treadmill for a senior have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              At least 50 lb more than the heaviest user. The picks here range from 245 lb on the Sunny low-deck treadmill to 400 lb on the Exerpeutic and 500 lb on the NuStep T5. A higher rating usually means a stiffer frame, which helps a cautious walker feel steady.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill on sale for Black Friday?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It was $339.15 on Amazon on 28 November 2026, about $60 under the $399 it showed on 6 November. Check the brand on the listing before you buy, because its title no longer starts with Exerpeutic and similar senior treadmills from other brands use near-identical titles.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are walking machines safe for people with balance problems?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A moving belt is a fall risk for anyone with poor balance, even with rails and a safety key. If you use a cane or walker, get dizzy on standing or have fallen in the past year, a seated cross trainer is the safer walking machine. Ask a physiotherapist before using any treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

